# VIP: view models live in SceneModels

`<Scene>Models` is an `enum` namespace. Scene view models, row models, and scene-only actions are nested **inside** it — never at module root.

```swift
enum HomeModels {
    enum FetchContent {
        enum ViewModel {
            case content(Content)
            case error
            struct Content {
                let featured: FeaturedViewModel
            }
        }
    }

    struct FeaturedViewModel {
        let title: String
    }
}
```

## Do

- Use-case envelope: `<Scene>Models.<UseCase>.Request` / `Response` / `ViewModel`
- Subview models: siblings of the use cases (`HomeModels.MovieCardViewModel`)
- Short names inside the namespace (`CastViewModel`, not `MovieDetailsCastViewModel`)
- Each scene owns its type even when fields match (`ProfileModels.StatsViewModel` vs `StatsModels.SummaryViewModel`)

## Shared UI

A view used by two or more scenes lives in `Presentation/UI/` and owns `SomeView.ViewModel`. Presenters emit scene models; the scene content view maps at the view boundary.

## Avoid

- `struct FeaturedViewModel` at file root next to `HomeModels`
- `struct HomeModels` (use `enum`)
- Stats importing `ProfileModels` (or the reverse) to reuse a view model
- Configuring `Presentation/UI/` views with another scene's nested type
